# gpu-pytorch-g4dn-2b — authoritative configuration.
#
# The directory name is documentation; this file decides. Never copy a slot value
# out of the directory name into a CLI flag (NAMING.md, "The name is a claim").
#
# Committed on purpose and deliberately NOT gitignored. A real environment
# variable always wins over it: mcp-run.sh exports only unset keys, Makefiles
# use ?=, and dotenv does not overwrite.
#
# STATUS: THIS RIG HAS SERVED NOTHING. Forked from `gpu-pytorch-g5g-2b` on
# 2026-08-29 and retargeted from Graviton2 (aarch64) to x86_64, keeping the
# runtime. `benchmarks/` is deliberately empty. There are NO MEASURED values in
# this file; every number below is either read from an AWS API, taken from a
# vendor spec, or inherited from another rig and labelled as such.
#
# WHERE THIS RIG SITS. Four rigs form a 2x2 over {runtime} x {host}:
#
#                     G5g (Graviton2 aarch64)   G4dn (x86_64)
#   pure JAX          gpu-jax-g5g-2b            gpu-jax-g4dn-2b
#   PyTorch           gpu-pytorch-g5g-2b        gpu-pytorch-g4dn-2b   <- here
#
# The GPU is the same Turing generation in both columns -- T4G on G5g, T4 on
# G4dn, both SM 7.5, and BOTH REPORTING 15360 MiB via nvidia-smi (not the 16384
# that describe_instance_types implies; measured on the JAX rig 2026-08-29).
# So the column is the HOST and the row is the RUNTIME.
#
# THE ROW IS ANSWERED. `gpu-jax-g4dn-2b` first served 2026-08-29 and landed on
# top of its G5g sibling: 13.1 tok/s against 13.10, weight bytes equal to the
# byte, and an xprof profile reproducing 54.4% dtype conversion / 32.8% fp32
# GEMV / 0.0% TensorCore with identical roofline peaks. The host contributes
# nothing measurable to decode.
#
# THE COLUMN IS WHAT THIS RIG IS FOR. That 86.9% tax is therefore a Turing
# property rather than a Graviton2 one, and the remaining question is whether it
# is the CHIP or XLA. `gpu-jax-g4dn-2b` is the same host, chip, checkpoint and
# dtype policy, so this is the cleanest runtime A/B in the tree. The baseline to
# beat or match: 13.1 / 13.2 / 13.1 tok/s at 41 / 521 / 2,057 input tokens.

# --- Model -------------------------------------------------------------------
# The reference instruction-tuned release, so the rig name carries no encoding
# slot. MODELS.md: 10.2 GB / 9.5 GiB of bf16 weights, 18 KiB/token of KV.
#
# Deliberately NOT the -qat-w4a16-ct export the TPU JAX rig serves, and the
# reason here is NOT the reason the JAX siblings give. Theirs is that the fused
# W4A16 Pallas kernel needs 550 KiB - 1.1 MiB of shared memory per block against
# Turing's 64 KiB. There is no Pallas in this rig and no fused path at all:
# `AutoModelForCausalLM` simply has nowhere to put a w4a16 checkpoint without
# bitsandbytes or torchao, neither of which is installed. Same outcome, different
# mechanism -- do not repeat the Pallas argument here.
MODEL_NAME=google/gemma-4-E2B-it

# Recorded, not read. Nothing in the serving path branches on it; it states that
# the checkpoint above is dense so a reader does not have to open it to find out.
QUANT_MODE=fp16

# --- Hardware ----------------------------------------------------------------
# The hardware slot is `g4dn` — the EC2 instance family, by the general EC2 rule
# in NAMING.md (widened from the `g5g` carve-out on 2026-08-28). The GPU is an
# NVIDIA T4; `t4` is not used as the slot because the family names the host
# architecture as well, which the SKU does not, and G4dn and G5g are the same
# Turing-class silicon on different hosts.
INSTANCE_TYPE=g4dn.xlarge
AWS_REGION=us-east-1

# Read from `ec2 describe-instance-types` on 2026-08-29, not from a product page.
#
#   size            GPUs  GPU mem  vCPU   RAM
#   g4dn.xlarge      1     16 GB     4    16 GB   <- default
#   g4dn.2xlarge     1     16 GB     8    32 GB
#   g4dn.4xlarge     1     16 GB    16    64 GB
#   g4dn.8xlarge     1     16 GB    32   128 GB
#   g4dn.12xlarge    4     64 GB    48   192 GB
#   g4dn.16xlarge    1     16 GB    64   256 GB
#   g4dn.metal       8    128 GB    96   384 GB
#
# THE "16 GB" COLUMN IS THE API'S NUMBER AND THE DEVICE DISAGREES. nvidia-smi
# reports 15360 MiB, IDENTICAL to the T4G, with 14.07 GiB usable -- measured on
# gpu-jax-g4dn-2b 2026-08-29, which had predicted a real 1 GiB advantage here on
# the strength of describe_instance_types and did not get one. Size a deployment
# from 14.07 GiB. The device is what allocates.
#
# THE LADDER IS NOT MONOTONIC IN THE SIZE SUFFIX: 16xlarge carries ONE T4 and
# 12xlarge carries four. Anything deriving GPU count from the number in the name
# is wrong on this family. Nor does vCPU track RAM the way it does on G5g, where
# vCPU = RAM/2 holds on every size; here it is RAM/4 throughout, which is why
# server.py carries vCPUs in the table rather than computing them.
#
# The 8 GiB host that forced a swapfile on the G5g sibling has no counterpart
# here -- g4dn starts at 16 GiB.

# Topology, not a sharding choice. The engine is single-device
# (`torch.device("cuda")`, index 0) and _serve_argv emits no tensor-parallel
# flag, so on 12xlarge three T4s idle and on metal seven do. A bigger instance
# buys host RAM and vCPUs, never device memory.
TENSOR_PARALLEL_SIZE=1

# --- Turing (SM 7.5) constraints ---------------------------------------------
# Properties of the chip, so they carry from the G5g rigs unchanged -- T4 and T4G
# are the same generation. The HOST facts from those rigs do not carry.
#
# Turing has no bf16 datapath. It is not a hard failure: CUDA accepts bfloat16
# and emulates it through fp32, so the numbers come out right while every matmul
# quietly pays. float16 is what actually executes on Turing tensor cores.
#
# THE DEVICE DECIDES, NOT THIS FILE. `resolve_compute_dtype()` in
# torch_openai_server.py reads the live compute capability and returns float16
# below SM 8.0. The value here is a record of that policy; setting it to
# bfloat16 would emulate rather than fail, which is the worse outcome.
DTYPE=float16

# Turing has no fp8 either. Unlike the JAX rigs there is no cache-dtype
# resolution step to refuse it: transformers owns the KV cache and allocates it
# in the model dtype, so `auto` is the only meaningful value and it follows
# DTYPE to float16. There is no int8 KV option on this path.
KV_CACHE_DTYPE=auto

# The static buffer length, passed as `--seq`. THIS IS THIS RIG'S ONE REAL
# SERVING KNOB and the only value here that reaches the process as a parameter
# rather than as a record.
#
# 4096 is INHERITED FROM THE JAX SIBLING AND ITS REASONING DOES NOT TRANSFER.
# There, 8192 was lowered to 4096 because a 5,120-token prompt failed allocating
# 2.59 GiB of prefill temporaries in the hand-written JAX prefill, and the
# documented mitigation was unreachable. This rig has none of that code:
# transformers prefills through SDPA and owns its own cache. So 4096 here is a
# CONSERVATIVE PLACEHOLDER carried across a fork, not a measured ceiling. It may
# well be low. Raise it with a measurement, not with an argument.
MAX_MODEL_LEN=4096

# One sequence. Not a tuning choice yet -- torch_openai_server.py serves one
# request at a time. Raising it needs batching in the server first.
MAX_NUM_SEQS=1

# --- Serving -----------------------------------------------------------------
SERVICE_NAME=torch-g4dn
TORCH_PORT=8000
HF_SECRET_ID=vllm/hf-token

# --- PyTorch runtime ---------------------------------------------------------
# TORCH IS NOT INSTALLED BY THIS RIG. It comes from the AMI, and that inverts the
# JAX siblings: there the DLAMI supplies only a driver because pip supplies CUDA;
# here the image supplies torch itself and the bootstrap installs INTO it.
# `install.sh` probes for the interpreter that can already `import torch` and
# writes the resolved path to APP_DIR/PYTHON_BIN, because the DLAMI ships a venv
# whose location moves between releases.
#
# So this is what goes on top, and nothing else:
TORCH_PIP_SPEC=transformers accelerate

# NOT the interpreter the service runs -- that is the DLAMI's venv, located by
# the probe. This only names the SYSTEM interpreter the bootstrap ensures exists
# so the probe has candidates on a non-PyTorch image.
#
# 3.14 HERE AND 3.12 ON THE G5G SIBLING, and the difference is forced by the AMI
# below rather than chosen. Ubuntu 26.04 ships 3.14 as its system python, so this
# stage is a no-op; deadsnakes publishes python3.14 for jammy and noble ONLY, so
# pinning 3.12 against a 26.04 image would miss the system interpreter, take the
# deadsnakes branch, and fail under `set -e` on a PPA with nothing to offer it.
# Move this and DLAMI_SSM_PARAMETER together.
TORCH_PYTHON_VERSION=3.14

# --- AMI ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# x86_64 PyTorch GPU DLAMI. Three independent requirements, and the third is the
# one that separates this rig from the JAX ones:
#   1. x86_64 — a Graviton image cannot boot here, and vice versa.
#   2. The NVIDIA driver.
#   3. TORCH ITSELF. A base driver-only DLAMI satisfies 1 and 2, boots fine, and
#      fails at install.sh's torch-interpreter stage — which reads as a broken
#      bootstrap rather than as the wrong image.
#
# `/latest/` in a DLAMI parameter path is only the newest build WITHIN that
# PyTorch-version + Ubuntu-version line, and AWS freezes lines it stops
# rebuilding. The version in the path is therefore a REAL PIN that has to be
# revisited; it does not track.
#
# VERIFIED 2026-08-29 by enumerating the 60 x86_64 DLAMI parameters:
# pytorch-2.13-ubuntu-26.04 is the newest line, it resolved to
# ami-0d7cd40a7956dd2c4 ("Deep Learning OSS Nvidia Driver AMI GPU PyTorch 2.13
# (Ubuntu 26.04) 20260829"), and its SSM entry had been rewritten that morning —
# a live line, not a frozen one.
#
# TWO THINGS ARE NEWER HERE THAN ON THE G5G SIBLING because x86_64 has them and
# arm64 does not: PyTorch 2.13 against its 2.12, and Ubuntu 26.04 against its
# 24.04. 26.04 is also what the JAX rigs settled on, and it is what makes
# TORCH_PYTHON_VERSION=3.14 above a no-op instead of a PPA round trip.
#
# NOT VERIFIED ON HARDWARE. Nothing has launched from this AMI. In particular
# the G5g rig's claim that "upstream PyPI wheels omit sm_75, so the AMI is the
# only source of a Turing torch" was MEASURED FOR AARCH64 and is NOT established
# for x86_64. Taking torch from the image is still right — vendor build, vendor
# driver — but do not repeat that argument here. `verify_gpu_arch` prints
# `torch.cuda.get_arch_list()` and runs a real fp16 matmul; it settles the
# question on any given image in one call.
DLAMI_SSM_PARAMETER=/aws/service/deeplearning/ami/x86_64/oss-nvidia-driver-gpu-pytorch-2.13-ubuntu-26.04/latest/ami-id

# Fallback for describe-images, and it had to be rewritten rather than carried
# over. AWS names the two architectures' images in DIFFERENT WORD ORDER:
#
#   arm64   Deep Learning ARM64 AMI OSS Nvidia Driver GPU PyTorch 2.12 (Ubuntu 24.04)
#   x86_64  Deep Learning OSS Nvidia Driver AMI GPU PyTorch 2.13 (Ubuntu 26.04)
#
# VERIFIED 2026-08-29: the G5g rig's pattern matches ZERO x86_64 images. Carried
# over unchanged it would have failed only when SSM was also unavailable, which
# is exactly when the fallback is load-bearing.
DLAMI_NAME=Deep Learning OSS Nvidia Driver AMI GPU PyTorch*Ubuntu*

# --- What is deliberately absent ---------------------------------------------
# NOT CARRIED FROM THE FORK, and none of it should come back without a reader:
#
#   PLE_BITS, INT8_LM_HEAD, PREFILL_CHUNK_SIZE
#       Knobs on `ports/gemma4/`, the JAX model port. This rig does not vendor
#       it. They were live constants in the rig this was forked from, rendered
#       into the systemd unit for a process that never read them.
#
#   XLA_PYTHON_CLIENT_MEM_FRACTION
#       XLA's device-memory preallocator. transformers allocates on demand.
#
#   JAX_COMPILATION_CACHE_DIR, JAX_CACHE_S3_URI, JAX_CACHE_SYNC_MINUTES
#       There is no torch.compile on this path, so nothing compiles and there is
#       no cache to warm, persist or sync. The fork shipped a systemd timer
#       pushing an empty directory to S3 every ten minutes and reporting success
#       — the same silent-success shape as the JAX rig's own cache bug. If
#       torch.compile is adopted here the knob is TORCHINDUCTOR_CACHE_DIR.
#
# The rule the absences share: a config key that nothing reads is worse than a
# missing one, because "the flag was accepted" then looks like evidence.
